# THE LITERAL TYPE CANNOT BE MADE. `intliteral` is what the checker gives a
# lexeme before context pins it, and its declaration says `create: null`, so a
# binding that names it bare is refused by the SAME rule that refuses any other
# non-constructible type -- see bare_create_null, where a user record does the
# identical thing. There is no literal-specific message and should not be one.
#
# The name resolves because `core` re-exports it, which is also what lets a
# unit-level alias (`n: intliteral`) name the real type rather than a shell.
main: function is {
    k: intliteral
    print "k=\{k.i64}"
}
